s to cut future repair costs but also accommodates the natural shifts of the island. While new parking arrangements will be established, the former site will transition back into its native ecosystem.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/in-2015-a-storm-flooded-and-destroyed-a-virginia-wildlife-refuge-beach-parking-lot-now-officials-are-moving-it-2-5-miles-north-so-dunes-and-saltmarsh-can-reclaim-the-old-site/articleshow/133350019.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/in-2015-a-storm-flooded-and-destroyed-a-virginia-wildlife-refuge-beach-parking-lot-now-officials-are-moving-it-2-5-miles-north-so-dunes-and-saltmarsh-can-reclaim-the-old-site/articleshow/133350019.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-19T18:59:26+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133350103,imgsize-2035626.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Bought in 2016, Indiana bishop’s former home with chapel is now listed at $1.28 million]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133348436.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[After nine years of dedication, an Indiana family has lovingly restored a historic home that once served as a Catholic bishop's residence, complete with a private chapel. Purchased at auction in 2016, they meticulously preserved its original features and completed extensive renovations. Now listed for $1.284 million, the family is ready to embark on new adventures and explore the world.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/real-estate/news/in-2016-an-indiana-family-bought-a-former-bishops-residence-with-its-own-private-chapel-after-nine-years-restoring-it-the-century-old-home-is-listed-for-1-28-million/articleshow/133348436.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/real-estate/news/in-2016-an-indiana-family-bought-a-former-bishops-residence-with-its-own-private-chapel-after-nine-years-restoring-it-the-century-old-home-is-listed-for-1-28-million/articleshow/133348436.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-19T18:02:05+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI Real Estate Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133348596,imgsize-1401055.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[European wild boars escaped a North Carolina hunting ranch in 1912; a century later...]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133347671.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[Invasive European wild boars, originally introduced to the area in 1912, are wreaking havoc within Great Smoky Mountains National Park. To mitigate damage to delicate ecosystems, park management annually removes around 275 of these feral hogs. The boars breed with domestic pigs, creating hybrid species that further disrupt native wildlife. Efforts to control their population involve trapping, shooting, and implementing fencing strategies.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/europe/european-wild-boars-escaped-a-north-carolina-hunting-ranch-in-1912-a-century-later-the-smokies-still-removes-hundreds-each-year-to-limit-their-damage/articleshow/133347671.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/europe/european-wild-boars-escaped-a-north-carolina-hunting-ranch-in-1912-a-century-later-the-smokies-still-removes-hundreds-each-year-to-limit-their-damage/articleshow/133347671.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-19T17:45:32+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133347927,imgsize-1002796.cms"/></item></channel></rss>